              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.5: Planetary model suggested by Rutherford"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.088,
            0.578,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A. Rutherford’s model of an atom"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.109,
            0.888,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the gold foil experiment, Rutherford concluded that the positive charge of an atom is not spread throughout but remains concentrated in an extremely small region called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "nucleus",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". He proposed that:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.164,
            0.888,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Most of an atom is empty space, as most α-particles passed through the gold foil without any deflection."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.233,
            0.201,
            0.888,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y The nucleus is dense, contains all the positive charge and most of the mass of an atom."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.239,
            0.89,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y The electrons revolve around the nucleus, somewhat like planets orbiting the Sun. Hence, this model is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "planetary model of the atom",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 8.5)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.293,
            0.89,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rutherford found that the nucleus is extremely small — about "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 0 ^ { 5 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (one lakh) times smaller than the atom. He calculated that the diameter of an atom is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\approx   1 0 ^ { - 1 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " m, and the diameter of the nucleus is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\approx   1 0 ^ { - 1 5 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " m. You can imagine that if an atom were of the size of a cricket ground (about 100 m across), the nucleus would be just a tiny black pepper grain (a few mm) at the centre!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.391,
            0.213,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.148,
                0.391,
                0.213,
                0.46
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.407,
            0.438,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.432,
            0.868,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you calculate how many atoms would be needed to make a sheet of paper that is 0.1 mm thick, like the one in your textbook?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.21,
            0.467,
            0.866,
            0.504
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If the diameter of one atom is about "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "10^{-10}    m ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and the sheet is 0.1 mm "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( 1 0 ^ { - 4 }   \\mathrm { m } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " thick, number of atoms "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\approx (10^{-4} \\mathrm{~m}) \\div (10^{-10} \\mathrm{~m}) = 10^6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.21,
            0.506,
            0.868,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "That is about one million atoms need to be stacked together! When these atoms come together, they form the solid materials we see and touch. What feels like a simple sheet is actually a vast assembly of tiny building blocks, all neatly arranged. It’s amazing how the unseen atomic world shapes everything we see and use every day!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.606,
            0.216,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.151,
                0.606,
                0.216,
                0.656
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_13.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.219,
            0.613,
            0.422,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think as a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.644,
            0.859,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe Fig. 8.4 of the gold foil experiment. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Predict",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the observations you would expect if the gold foil in the experiment were made thicker. Also, draw a simple diagram to show the observations you expect."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.693,
            0.859,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint: Compare thin foil vs thick foil. How does the thickness affect the chances of hitting a nucleus?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.76,
            0.248,
            0.787
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.191,
                0.76,
                0.248,
                0.787
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_17.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.259,
            0.768,
            0.449,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.794,
            0.858,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. What do you think would happen if α-particles were replaced with negatively charged particles in Rutherford’s gold foil experiment?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.83,
            0.858,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Rutherford found that a few α-particles bounced back sharply. How does this single surprising result completely rule out Thomson’s ʻplum pudding modelʼ of the atom?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.88,
            0.839,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. If you could ask Rutherford one question about his work, what would it be?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 22,
